# d3Sea — Muireann on the north headland
#
#   node 0  THE MEETING   @when !note met_muireann — seen once
#   node 1  THE HUB       @hold — where she lives for the rest of the game
#
# WHY HER QUESTION IS IN NODE 0
#   A hub node's text is shown on arrival and then replaced by @again, so
#   it cannot hold a question the player answers later — the answers would
#   sit in the list with nothing to attach to. Her question therefore ends
#   the MEETING, inside a real exchange, and hangs over the hub until the
#   player gets to it. Both meeting paths ask it, so both open the answers.
#
# GATING CONSTRAINT
#   requires holds ONE positive note and ONE negative note per option. A
#   second @when note X overwrites the first. Questions that need two
#   things to have happened are chained instead:
#
#     met_muireann      -> her question has been asked; answers available
#     knows_fionnbarra  -> opens "Who is Fionnbarra?"
#     knows_of_tollman  -> opens "His toll-man?" and "His hounds?"
#
# OTHER RULES
#   One @note per option. @silent = no reply card, never on an answer.
#   An @exit option's reply is never shown. One line is one line.

@file d3Sea


# ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
= node 0 — the meeting
@when !note met_muireann
Who art thou that rows so bold
% Cé thú féin atá ag rámhíocht go cróga
towards the mouth of the Boyne?
% i dtreo béal na Bóinne?

* I am...
% Is mise...
@easca playerName
@note met_muireann
> I am {playerName} 
% Is mise {playerName}
> Blessing of the wind to you.
% Beannacht na gaoithe ort.
< Blessing of the shore to you, {playerName}.
% Beannacht an claddach ort, a {playerName}.
< Muireann is my name.
% Muireann is ainm dom.
< And where are you going?
% Agus cá bhfuil do thríal?

* Give no name
% Ná tabhair ainm
@note withheld_name
> My quarrel is with my own name, no deed of mine has given it meaning.
% Is le m'ainm féin mo chomhrac; níor thug beart de mo chuid brí dó.

< Is that so.
% Ab ea.
< Then answer me this instead.
% Freagair seo dom mar sin.
< where are you going?
% Agus cá bhfuil do thríall?

* leave
% fág
@exit
@silent


# ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
= node 1 — the hub
@hold
@again And? / Agus?
...
% ...

# ── answering her ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# She asked at the meeting and has not been answered. These sit at the
# top of the list until one is chosen; each then hides the other for good.

* Tell her
% Abair lei
@when !note evaded_muireann
@first
@note told_muireann_truth
> To the bog that drinks men whole, to Allen's sunken ground.
% Chun na móna ina chailtear fir, chuig talamh báite Almhain.
> Where the Fianna test their own.
% Áit a thriailfar na Fhian.
< The bog will challenge thee.
% Cuirfidh an mhóin dúthlán ort.
< Hast thou mastered the spear?
% An bhfuil an gha fhoghlamaíthe agat?


* Tell half truth
% Inis leath fhírinne
@when !note told_muireann_truth
@first
@note evaded_muireann
> Ask the heron of the fen. She will know my story.
% Íarr ar chorr na móna. Is aici a bheidh mo scéal.
< Speak plainly. What business brings a stranger to these lands?
% Freagair go díreach mé. Cén gnó a thóg chuig na tailte seo thú?
> The wind does not explain itself to the standing stone.
% Ní mhíníonn an ghaoth í féin don ghallán.
< Some purpose drives thee, I think.
% Tá rún i do chroí dod brú ar aghaidh, sílim.

# Each answers what she said above it, so both stay contextual.

* I will learn from Fionn 
% Foghlaimoidh mé ó Fhionn
@when note told_muireann_truth
@first


> Fionn will teach me what the bog does not.
% Múinfidh Fionn dom an rud nach múineann an mhóin.
< I know Fionn. Fionn the great one.
% Tá aithne agam ar Fionn \n an gaiscíoch mór.

<Fionn keeps poetry before the blade.
% Is uaisle le Fionn an fhilíocht ná an slea.

* Say little
% Ná habair murán
@when note evaded_muireann
@first
> There is a thing that waits, and it is not yet found.
% Tá rud ag feitheamh orm, nach bhfuil feicthe fós.
< Go then.
% Imigh leat mar sin.


# ── what she knows ────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Chained, so nothing can be asked about before it has been mentioned.

* Where am I?
% Cá bhfuil mé?
@when !note knows_fionnbarra
@note knows_fionnbarra
> Whose shore is this?
% Cé leis an trá seo?
< Thy little boat has reached the mouth of Bóinn the bright and winding.
% Tháinig do bháidín go béal na Bóinne ghlé ghroí.
< This shore belongs first to her.
% Is léisí an trá seo ar dtús.
< Fionnbarra mac Dubhloingse is Chief of this valley
% Is é Fionnbarra mac Dubhloingse Taoiseach an gleann seo.


* Fionnbarra?
% Fionnbarra?
@when note knows_fionnbarra
@when !note knows_of_tollman
@note knows_of_tollman
> What kind of man is Fionnbarra?
% Cén cineál duine é Fionnbarra?
< Fionnbarra is not an unjust man.
% Ní fear héagórach é Fionnbarra.
< His hounds are many.
% Is iomaí cú atá aige.
< But his toll-man waits at the river and his face like an angry stone.
% Ach tá a fhear cána ag feitheamh ag an abhainn agus aghaidh mar carraig crosta aige.

* His hounds?
% A chuid con?
@when note knows_of_tollman
@when !note asked_hounds
@note asked_hounds
> Tell me of his hounds.
% Inis dom faoina madraí.
< Brocán and Dorchán,
% Brocán agus Dorchán,
< and the grey one they call Scáthán.
% agus an ceann liath ar a dtugtar Scáthán.

< It is a dangerous curiosity.
% Is baolach an fhiosracht é.
< Do not think of them,
% Ná smaoinigh orthu,
< lest they hear the thought in thy skull
% ar eagla go gcloisfidís an smaoineamh i do chloigeann
< and come for thee in the darkness.
% agus go dtiocfaidís ort sa dorchadas.

* His toll-man?
% A fhear cána?
@when note knows_of_tollman
@when !note withheld_name
@note has_druid_word
> His toll-man?
% A fhear cána?
< Such is how he does business:
% Mar seo a dhéanann sé gnó:
< whom he robs not, he delays.
% an té nach ngoidfidh sé uaidh, cuirfidh sé moill air.
< If Fionnbarra's toll-man should meet thee at the strand,
% Má chasann fear cána Fhionnbarra ort ar an gcladach,
< thou art to say that Muireann the Druid knows thy name.
% abair leis go bhfuil d'ainm ag Muireann an Draoi.

* His toll-man?
% A fhear cána?
@when note knows_of_tollman
@when !note met_muireann
> His toll-man?
% A fhear cána?
< Such is how he does business:
% Mar seo a dhéanann sé gnó:
< whom he robs not, he delays.
% an té nach ngoidfidh sé uaidh, cuirfidh sé moill air.

# ── the road ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Never gated away: a player who has forgotten the way must be able to ask
# again. The pipes warning lives here because it is travel advice, and
# because an @exit option's reply would never be shown.

* Which way?
% Cén treo?
@note knows_rath
> Which way, then?
% Cén treo, mar sin?
< Half a morning's walk northwest along the track. Thou wilt see the ringfort.
% Siúil siar ó thuaidh feadh an bhealaigh leath na maidine. Feicfidh tú an ráth.
< Thou wilt find food, shelter, the hospitality of Fionnbarra.
% Gheobhaidh tú bia, dídean, agus fáilte Fhionnbarra ann.
> And after?
% Agus ina dhiaidh sin?
< That great dark place, with water the colour of old bronze.
% An áit mhór dhorcha sin, agus uisce uirthi ar dhath an tsean-chré-umha.
< It is not far. Two days west, two days south. Keep the hills to thy left hand.
% Níl sé i bhfad. Dhá lá siar, dhá lá ó dheas. Coinnigh na cnoic ar do láimh chlé.
< Go not by night.
% Ná téigh is d'oíche.
< And if thou hear the sound of pipes where no one stands,
% Agus má chloiseann tú ceol na bpíob gan seinnteoir,
< thou hast heard nothing. Walk on.
% níor chuala tú faic. Lean ort.
< May thy journey succeed.
% Go n-éirí an bóthar leat.

* Row on
% Fág
@exit
@silent
